    },
    {
      "id": "6rnHhhEgyvVao7RaPwr-R",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 821.9913462688216,
      "y": 8915.280910010892,
      "width": 1177,
      "height": 1050,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1971c2",
      "backgroundColor": "#ffc9c9",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "b46",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 1538896170,
      "version": 198,
      "versionNonce": 1705540971,
      "isDeleted": true,
      "boundElements": [],
      "updated": 1788288733999,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "Imagine:\n    python app.py\n\nPython finishes.\nYou think:\nProcess disappears.\nNot immediately.\n\nEvery process has a parent.\nWhen the child exits:\n\nLinux keeps a tiny record containing:\n\nExit status\nResource usage\nPID\n\nWhy?\nBecause the parent may want to know:\nDid my child succeed?\n\nThe zombie occupies almost no memory and uses no CPU, but it still has a process table entry \nuntil the parent collects its exit status (using a system call like wait()).\n\n\nWhy Do Zombies Become a Problem?\n    One zombie? -> No issue.\n    Imagine: -> 50,000 Zombies\n    Every zombie occupies a process table slot.\n    Eventually:\n    Linux cannot create new processes.\n\nInterview Question\n\n1 Can a zombie be killed?\n    No. It's already dead.\n    The correct solution is:\n\n    The parent process calls wait() to reap it, or\n    If the parent has exited, PID 1 (systemd on most systems) adopts and reaps it\n\n    Sending kill -9 to a zombie doesn't help because there's no running process left to receive the signal.",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "fontFamily": 8,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "Imagine:\n    python app.py\n\nPython finishes.\nYou think:\nProcess disappears.\nNot immediately.\n\nEvery process has a parent.\nWhen the child exits:\n\nLinux keeps a tiny record containing:\n\nExit status\nResource usage\nPID\n\nWhy?\nBecause the parent may want to know:\nDid my child succeed?\n\nThe zombie occupies almost no memory and uses no CPU, but it still has a process table entry \nuntil the parent collects its exit status (using a system call like wait()).\n\n\nWhy Do Zombies Become a Problem?\n    One zombie? -> No issue.\n    Imagine: -> 50,000 Zombies\n    Every zombie occupies a process table slot.\n    Eventually:\n    Linux cannot create new processes.\n\nInterview Question\n\n1 Can a zombie be killed?\n    No. It's already dead.\n    The correct solution is:\n\n    The parent process calls wait() to reap it, or\n    If the parent has exited, PID 1 (systemd on most systems) adopts and reaps it\n\n    Sending kill -9 to a zombie doesn't help because there's no running process left to receive the signal.",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
    {
      "id": "CXM5UrqHzQt1zlq3WImV7",
