      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": null,
      "updated": 1788290202491,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "10. Heap",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "fontFamily": 8,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "10. Heap",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
    {
      "id": "FhWT7vT-oBzGXeIjz-lac",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 688.2121521898734,
      "y": 12455.422768563432,
      "width": 891,
      "height": 650,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1971c2",
      "backgroundColor": "#ffc9c9",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 0,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "b50",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 1691900005,
      "version": 94,
      "versionNonce": 355455813,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": null,
      "updated": 1788290297825,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "The heap is used for dynamically allocated memory.\nPython makes heavy use of dynamic memory.\nFor example:\n\nusers = []\n\nThen:\n\nusers.append(\"Amit\")\nusers.append(\"Rahul\")\nusers.append(\"Rishabh\")\n\nObjects created during execution require memory.\nConceptually:\n\nHeap\n│\n├── Python objects\n├── Lists\n├── Dictionaries\n├── Strings\n├── Django objects\n└── Other dynamically allocated data\n\nThis is why a Python application's memory usage can grow significantly depending \non what objects it keeps alive.",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "fontFamily": 8,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "The heap is used for dynamically allocated memory.\nPython makes heavy use of dynamic memory.\nFor example:\n\nusers = []\n\nThen:\n\nusers.append(\"Amit\")\nusers.append(\"Rahul\")\nusers.append(\"Rishabh\")\n\nObjects created during execution require memory.\nConceptually:\n\nHeap\n│\n├── Python objects\n├── Lists\n├── Dictionaries\n├── Strings\n├── Django objects\n└── Other dynamically allocated data\n\nThis is why a Python application's memory usage can grow significantly depending \non what objects it keeps alive.",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
    {
      "id": "Kc4EvAGIeTtR4dkaivo3Y",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 904.5637146898734,
