      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": null,
      "updated": 1788290318709,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "11. Stack vs Heap",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "fontFamily": 8,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "11. Stack vs Heap",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
    {
      "id": "yQhZWZINMeFZ_GaFEjkJ1",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 696.4348084398734,
      "y": 13211.399331063432,
      "width": 1133,
      "height": 325,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1971c2",
      "backgroundColor": "#ffc9c9",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 0,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "b53",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 2046585643,
      "version": 15,
      "versionNonce": 254658475,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": null,
      "updated": 1788290371328,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "This is a common interview topic.\n\n| Stack                                     | Heap                                    |\n| ----------------------------------------- | --------------------------------------- |\n| Function-call information                 | Dynamically allocated objects           |\n| Usually smaller                           | Usually much larger                     |\n| Automatically managed as calls return     | Objects remain until released/reclaimed |\n| Very fast access                          | More flexible                           |\n| Stores stack frames/local execution state | Stores dynamically created data         |\n\n\nFor Python specifically, don't overgeneralize the C/C++ model: Python objects themselves generally \nlive in heap-managed memory, while the Python interpreter uses its own structures for execution frames.",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "fontFamily": 8,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "This is a common interview topic.\n\n| Stack                                     | Heap                                    |\n| ----------------------------------------- | --------------------------------------- |\n| Function-call information                 | Dynamically allocated objects           |\n| Usually smaller                           | Usually much larger                     |\n| Automatically managed as calls return     | Objects remain until released/reclaimed |\n| Very fast access                          | More flexible                           |\n| Stores stack frames/local execution state | Stores dynamically created data         |\n\n\nFor Python specifically, don't overgeneralize the C/C++ model: Python objects themselves generally \nlive in heap-managed memory, while the Python interpreter uses its own structures for execution frames.",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
    {
      "id": "SupHFJgaZoSR2UneJGvq2",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 650.5637146898734,
      "y": 13615.399331063432,
      "width": 352,
      "height": 25,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "#ffc9c9",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 0,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "b54",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 459129739,
      "version": 4,
      "versionNonce": 179096427,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": null,
      "updated": 1788290379642,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "12. Let's Connect This to Django",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "fontFamily": 8,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "12. Let's Connect This to Django",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
    {
      "id": "8zirt_3eQF9_KZdtRtCXx",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 683.5637146898734,
      "y": 13652.399331063432,
      "width": 583,
      "height": 850,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1971c2",
      "backgroundColor": "#ffc9c9",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 0,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "b55",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 287910469,
      "version": 25,
      "versionNonce": 318716619,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": null,
      "updated": 1788290517819,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "Suppose you have:\n\nDjango\n |\n ├── Python interpreter\n ├── Django framework\n ├── Imported libraries\n ├── Database connection objects\n ├── Request objects\n ├── Query results\n └── Application objects\n\nAll of these consume memory.\nImagine your API does:\n\nusers = User.objects.all()\n\nand then loads a huge number of objects into memory.\nYou could suddenly have:\n\nDjango Worker\n\nRAM usage\n   ↑\n   |\n   |       █\n   |      ██\n   |     ███\n   |    ████\n   |   █████\n   +----------------> time\n\nThis becomes important later when we discuss:\nGunicorn workers + Docker + Kubernetes memory limits.",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "fontFamily": 8,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "Suppose you have:\n\nDjango\n |\n ├── Python interpreter\n ├── Django framework\n ├── Imported libraries\n ├── Database connection objects\n ├── Request objects\n ├── Query results\n └── Application objects\n\nAll of these consume memory.\nImagine your API does:\n\nusers = User.objects.all()\n\nand then loads a huge number of objects into memory.\nYou could suddenly have:\n\nDjango Worker\n\nRAM usage\n   ↑\n   |\n   |       █\n   |      ██\n   |     ███\n   |    ████\n   |   █████\n   +----------------> time\n\nThis becomes important later when we discuss:\nGunicorn workers + Docker + Kubernetes memory limits.",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
    {
      "id": "kJHf6mheoyDIe6fDpTQuL",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 638.5637146898734,
      "y": 14568.399331063432,
      "width": 209,
      "height": 25,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "#ffc9c9",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 0,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "b56",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 1529228357,
      "version": 4,
      "versionNonce": 1816229477,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": null,
      "updated": 1788291180564,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "13. What is a Page?",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "fontFamily": 8,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "13. What is a Page?",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
    {
      "id": "pL2OO-2E0UeH-V25P0CBv",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 684.5637146898734,
      "y": 14605.883706063432,
      "width": 737,
      "height": 700,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1971c2",
      "backgroundColor": "#ffc9c9",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 0,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "b57",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 1418183243,
      "version": 35,
      "versionNonce": 458327339,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": null,
      "updated": 1788291260930,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "Linux doesn't normally manage memory as one giant continuous chunk.\nMemory is divided into fixed-size pieces called pages.\nA common page size is: 4 KB\n\nSo instead of thinking: Process needs 100 MB\n\nthe OS can think:\n\nPage\nPage\nPage\nPage\nPage\n...\n\nThe virtual memory system maps these pages to physical memory.\n\nConceptually:\n\nVirtual Memory\n\nPage 1 ──────────┐\nPage 2 ──────────┤\nPage 3 ──────────┼──> Physical RAM\nPage 4 ──────────┤\nPage 5 ──────────┘\n\nThis page-based system is fundamental to Linux memory management.",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "fontFamily": 8,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "Linux doesn't normally manage memory as one giant continuous chunk.\nMemory is divided into fixed-size pieces called pages.\nA common page size is: 4 KB\n\nSo instead of thinking: Process needs 100 MB\n\nthe OS can think:\n\nPage\nPage\nPage\nPage\nPage\n...\n\nThe virtual memory system maps these pages to physical memory.\n\nConceptually:\n\nVirtual Memory\n\nPage 1 ──────────┐\nPage 2 ──────────┤\nPage 3 ──────────┼──> Physical RAM\nPage 4 ──────────┤\nPage 5 ──────────┘\n\nThis page-based system is fundamental to Linux memory management.",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
    {
      "id": "yW4UJmAVbbp_zM4o-k7sl",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 612.5637146898734,
      "y": 15356.399331063432,
      "width": 539,
      "height": 25,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "#ffc9c9",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 0,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "b58",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 1504211211,
      "version": 4,
      "versionNonce": 1866754283,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": null,
      "updated": 1788291268116,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "14. Why Pages Are Important for Docker/Kubernetes",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "fontFamily": 8,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "14. Why Pages Are Important for Docker/Kubernetes",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
    {
      "id": "EYpCYY4zTYSBiXuS3f9xi",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 649.5637146898734,
      "y": 15388.278237313432,
      "width": 891,
      "height": 525,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1971c2",
      "backgroundColor": "#ffc9c9",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 0,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "b59",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 473520485,
      "version": 19,
      "versionNonce": 83134987,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": null,
      "updated": 1788291307045,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "Later we'll configure something like:\n\nresources:\n  limits:\n    memory: \"512Mi\"\n\nKubernetes isn't magically giving your application exactly 512 MB of special RAM.\nIt is enforcing a memory resource limit through Linux's underlying mechanisms.\nEventually we'll connect:\n\nKubernetes\n     ↓\nContainer\n     ↓\nLinux cgroups\n     ↓\nMemory accounting/limits\n     ↓\nPhysical memory\n\nSo understanding Linux memory first makes Kubernetes much easier.",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "fontFamily": 8,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "Later we'll configure something like:\n\nresources:\n  limits:\n    memory: \"512Mi\"\n\nKubernetes isn't magically giving your application exactly 512 MB of special RAM.\nIt is enforcing a memory resource limit through Linux's underlying mechanisms.\nEventually we'll connect:\n\nKubernetes\n     ↓\nContainer\n     ↓\nLinux cgroups\n     ↓\nMemory accounting/limits\n     ↓\nPhysical memory\n\nSo understanding Linux memory first makes Kubernetes much easier.",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
    {
      "id": "TwRAPZ_TmhK09C-sw8q_J",
